# 6. ADVANCED SECURITY CENTER

Create a coherent security experience for Website and Android.

Evaluate the following capabilities.

## 6.1 Two-Factor Authentication

Support server-verified two-factor authentication where appropriate.

Possible methods:

- Time-based one-time passwords (TOTP)
- Authenticator applications
- Recovery or backup codes
- Additional verification for sensitive account operations

TOTP compatibility should follow the standard so compatible applications such as Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, and other standards-compliant authenticators can be supported.

## 6.2 Authenticator Setup Flow

A secure setup flow should:

1. Require an authenticated account.
2. Reauthenticate the user when appropriate.
3. Request server-generated TOTP enrollment information.
4. Display the provisioning QR code securely.
5. Permit manual entry where appropriate.
6. Verify an authenticator-generated code.
7. Enable 2FA only after successful server verification.
8. Generate backup codes securely.
9. Display backup codes only when appropriate and instruct the user to store them safely.
10. Record relevant security events without recording secrets or OTP values.

Never enable 2FA merely because the user opened the setup screen.

Never expose TOTP seeds or recovery codes in ordinary logs, analytics, crash reports, or unprotected persistent storage.

## 6.3 Recovery and Disablement

Design secure procedures for:

- Lost authenticator devices
- Backup-code use
- Recovery-code regeneration
- Changing authentication methods
- Disabling 2FA
- Account recovery
- Reauthentication before sensitive changes

Do not create an insecure bypass that allows a user to defeat 2FA through an unverified request.

## 6.4 Additional Security Capabilities

Evaluate these features:

- Active session list
- Session revocation
- Logout all devices
- New-device notifications
- Login alerts
- Suspicious-login detection
- Security activity history
- Reauthentication for sensitive actions
- Rate limiting
- Brute-force protection
- Recovery workflow protection
- CSRF protection
- Secure cookies and session handling
- Token expiry and revocation
- Appropriate audit logging

Only implement risk detection appropriate to available data and architecture. Do not claim sophisticated fraud detection exists when the project has only simple rules.

## 6.5 Android Biometrics Are Not Server-Side 2FA

Maintain the distinction between:

- Local biometric authentication
- Server-side account authentication
- Server-side two-factor authentication

Android biometrics may protect a locally stored credential or unlock an application session, but they must not automatically be treated as proof that the server has completed a second authentication factor.

Use Android Keystore and appropriate platform authentication controls when needed. Avoid storing reusable plaintext passwords.

Review the existing biometric implementation before modifying it.

